it's a labor-intensive process  counting by hand bullets from nearly 2  000 polling stations across several iraq  provinces  this came about after supporters of  political parties that lost seats  demanded our account  the representatives have been monitoring  the process  today we've recounted over a hundred  stations we are comparing current and  old results to see if there is a  difference between manual and electronic  numbers  in this case we go by manual count then  we'll announce the new results day by  day there is more than 50 observers here  iraq's parliamentary election earlier  this month which was moved up to meet  the demands of protesters  so a turnout of 41 percent  that's the lowest figure in the  post-saddam hussein era  the block affiliated with the  influential shia leader mukhtar  173 seats the most of any party  however the pro-iranian multi-party  al-fatiha alliance  secured only 17 seats down from 48 in  the last election several previously  dominant political parties have rejected  the election results  accusing the electoral commission of  vote rigging  the commission says  it has received more than 1400 appeals  so far  but it says  it has turned down most of them because  they're not supported by  for evidence than a week  supporters of the losing parties have  been protesting near the entrance to  baghdad's green zone  most of these protesters are members of  the iran-backed popular mobilization  forces  the military arm of the shia al-fatiha  alliance  security measures have been tightened  around the area which is home to the u.s  embassy  other international diplomatic missions  and government offices that followed  threats made by the protesters to storm  into the green zone  they have denounced the u.n mission  which monitored the election  and accused it of facilitating fraud  two thousand polling stations will not  change much we want manual recount of  all votes in all provinces in the  presence of neutral international  observers otherwise there will be  escalations and will demand the  re-election many people also say the  recount will not change much  supporters of the popular mobilization  forces have organized sporadic  protesters across the country in recent  days  they burned tires and blocked roads  north and south of baghdad  for them the election result is crucial  for their future  and if their political parties can't get  into parliament  they fear they'll have nobody to look  out for their interests  al jazeera baghdad
 

